bandwidth-logger (1.2.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Add a Test server setting. The engine chooses a server by lowest latency,
    which is not the same as fastest: measured on one gigabit line, the
    lowest-latency server returned 696 Mbps while another 0.5 ms further away
    returned 926. Left automatic the engine may also choose differently
    between runs, so a change in a recorded speed could mean only that a
    different server answered -- which defeats the purpose of keeping a
    history.
  * Settings can now fetch the list of nearby servers and pin one. Fetching
    contacts Ookla, so it happens only when asked and never during a
    scheduled test.
  * A pinned server id is validated as numeric before use.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.1.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * Remove the speedtest-cli fallback engine. It under-reported throughput
    badly on fast connections -- roughly half the real figure on a gigabit
    line -- because it cannot open enough parallel connections to fill one.
    For an application whose purpose is diagnosing a slow connection, a
    number that is quietly wrong by half is worse than no number at all.
  * Conflict with speedtest-cli rather than depending on it. Ubuntu's package
    owns /usr/bin/speedtest, the same path Ookla's package installs to, so
    having it present made the Ookla engine impossible to install.
  * Correct the Ookla install commands. Ookla publishes no repository for
    Ubuntu 24.04, so the upstream script must be told to use the jammy
    repository, which runs correctly on 24.04. The commands now also remove
    the conflicting package first. Verified working end to end.
  * Annotate stored results measured by the removed engine, so an old low
    reading cannot be misread as a real slowdown. No record is rewritten or
    deleted.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 13:00:00 +0000

bandwidth-logger (1.0.0-1) noble; urgency=medium

  * First release.
  * Manual and scheduled internet speed tests with a complete local history.
  * Every attempted test is recorded, including failures, timeouts and
    attempts skipped because another test was still running.
  * Background scheduling through a systemd user timer, so testing continues
    with the window closed and resumes after login.
  * Full and date-range CSV export, with the external IP address excluded by
    default.
  * Ookla Speedtest CLI is used when installed; the open-source speedtest-cli
    is a package dependency so the application works on first launch.

 -- Bandwidth Logger contributors <bandwidth-logger@example.invalid>  Tue, 04 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000
